  "worked_solution": "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)={\\begin{aligned}0\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,t<1 \\\\ -7\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{for}\\,\\,t>3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this definite integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{s}{e}^{-st}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s}).\n$$",
